<p>Thirty-six countries and the European Union said Friday they intend to join a Council of Europe-backed special tribunal to prosecute the crime of aggression against Ukraine, after the Council’s Committee of Ministers approved a resolution at a meeting in Chisinau, Moldova. The participants are 34 Council of Europe member states, plus Australia and Costa Rica, with the EU joining as an institution. The planned tribunal follows a 2025 accord signed by Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy and the Council of Europe to create a legal body addressing Russia’s February 2022 invasion, which supporters describe as the crime of aggression. Supporters say the body would address a jurisdictional gap because the International Criminal Court can investigate war crimes, crimes against humanity and genocide in Ukraine but cannot prosecute the crime of aggression in this case under its current rules. The tribunal could in theory try senior Russian officials, including President Vladimir Putin, but DW reported it remains unclear how it would compel Russian defendants to appear.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/E8W6O6tlivz0mry0UAJ9SxkRq2w8CFSagvZXCtO9fYY9jHZqOAuMd5qXxA322orZwGo-7m4AOg8NpzI_E7rTN7rYJLpnM9oTfFP0GEGrdg0HD1a_hNK7nqwWb2ixgDbSv2akeM30Ys9ZA_Fu_iNQDedKmuiLoKYm40kmScXvjMoGWfnseN7gX_eO5je7UfEB6sSm7eZ5qdWv_7Wv8dOWhzQVORje4O1MGgg-BXcz7h5b8Oc4E0v9EsWMyArHZr589cpp' alt='The Council of Europe approved the basis for a future special tribunal to try Russia&#x27;s aggression against Ukraine.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Compensation track: At the same ministerial meeting, ministers welcomed broad support for a mechanism created to provide compensation for Ukrainians.</li><li>Nonparticipating states: Twelve Council of Europe members had not joined the arrangement, including EU members Hungary, Slovakia, Bulgaria and Malta.</li><li>EU financing: The European Union provided an initial €10 million in January to support tribunal operations, STVR reported.</li><li>Next steps: Ukrainian official Iryna Mudra said the next phase would include selecting judges, approving procedures and opening investigations, potentially next year in The Hague.</li><li>Kyiv attack: The Council of Europe action came after a Russian strike on a central Kyiv apartment block killed 24 people, an attack Ukraine’s Foreign Ministry described as one of the deadliest since the war began.</li></ul><blockquote>The Special Tribunal represents justice and hope - Alain Berset</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Council of Europe: Secretary General Alain Berset urged states to complete the domestic procedures needed to join the tribunal and said operational funding must still be secured. (<a href='https://www.forum24.cz/rusko-ma-za-agresi-vuci-ukrajine-celit-zvlastnimu-tribunalu-k-iniciative-se-prihlasilo-i-cesko'>Forum24</a>)</li><li>Ukraine: Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha said the crime of aggression against Ukraine must be punished before what he called the moral foundation of Europe and the world could be restored. (<a href='https://www.eunews.it/en/2026/05/15/ukraine-council-of-europe-steps-up-push-on-special-tribunal-for-the-crime-of-aggression/'>EUnews</a>)</li><li>Estonia: Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna said broad support showed the international community wanted Putin and his associates brought before a court. (<a href='https://vm.ee/uudised/valisminister-tsahkna-rahvusvaheline-kogukond-andnud-selge-sonumi-et-soovib-naha-putinit-ja'>Estonian Foreign Ministry</a>)</li><li>Norway: Foreign Minister Espen Barth Eide said Ukraine was fighting for principles of European security, including that borders cannot be changed by force. (<a href='https://www.nrk.no/nyheter/etablerer-spesialtribunal-for-ukraina-1.17885584'>NRK</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-dozens-of-european-countries-sign-up-to-special-tribunal-plan-to-prosecute-russia/a-77174512'>Ukraine: Dozens of European countries sign up to special tribunal plan 